    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["What is the formula for cap rate?", "Cap Rate = Net Operating Income (NOI) A Property Value × 100. NOI equals gross rental income minus operating expenses — property taxes, insurance, management, maintenance, vacancy allowance. Mortgage payments and capital expenditures are excluded from operating expenses."], ["Is a higher cap rate always better?", "No. Higher cap rates typically signal higher risk — older buildings, less stable tenants, weaker appreciation markets. A 5% cap rate on a brand-new Class A apartment in a growing city can be a better investment than a 9% cap rate on a 1960s Class C building in a declining neighborhood. Always evaluate cap rate in the context of location, asset quality, and market fundamentals."], ["Does cap rate include mortgage payments?", "No. Mortgage principal and interest are explicitly excluded from cap rate calculations. Cap rate measures the property's performance independent of how it's financed. Including debt service invalidates the metric. To evaluate returns on your actual cash investment after mortgage payments, use <a href=\"/real-estate/cash-on-cash-return-calculator\">cash-on-cash return</a>."], ["Should I use purchase price or current market value?", "Current market value is the professional standard. Using an old purchase price produces a cap rate that reflects historical conditions, not today's market. Current value tells you the opportunity cost — what your capital could earn if invested elsewhere today. Use purchase price only when making a buy/don't-buy decision before closing."], ["What is the difference between cap rate and ROI?", "Cap rate is a snapshot metric — it measures one year of NOI versus current property value, ignoring financing, taxes, and appreciation. ROI is the full picture, including your actual cash invested, mortgage costs, tax benefits, appreciation over time, and eventual sale proceeds. Cap rate is the opening screening question; ROI is the final answer."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
